# Graph reachability in lambda plus merge, with no label: scored points of
# the label-free family.
#
# THE PROGRAM. A graph of N nodes, its edges in a fixed order:
# - node i is the Scott unary numeral i, let bound once as the successor
#   of node i - 1, so that the surface term grows linearly with N + E (an
#   N-ary selector per node would make it grow with N^2, and the judge's
#   translation of a 384-node program took minutes before any reading).
#   The IR does not grow linearly: flat closure conversion makes every
#   closure capture all the node variables in scope, and the IR grows by
#   about 3.3 a doubling (measured);
# - a table over the nodes is a nested case on the numeral,
#   v C0 (\p. p C1 (\p. ... )), Ci the case of node i;
# - reach(v) is the merge [is_target v, successors v], where the case of
#   node i in successors is the merge of reach applied to each successor
#   of node i, in the edges' order, and in is_target the Church Boolean,
#   true at the target;
# - reach is recursive by self-application, and the test reads the Church
#   Boolean of reach(start).
# Only Booleans are merged. The test reads true when some chain of choices
# reaches the target, which it does at a finite depth. Along every cycle
# the search goes on without end: a reading must find the yielding branch
# beside the ones that never stop, the ground of the labeled Datalog ring
# points, here without labels. The edge back into a cycle comes before the
# edge that leaves it, so a reading that tries the first operand first,
# and never gives it up, runs around the cycle forever.
#
# THE GRAPHS:
# - two rings of n nodes each: ring A from the start, ring B holding the
#   target at its last node; A's last node goes first back to A's first,
#   then over to B's first;
# - a ring of n nodes with chords: node i goes first back to node i - 1
#   (node 0 has no chord), then on to node i + 1, and the last node back
#   to node 0; the target is the last node.
# The reference is breadth-first reachability (reference_reachable/4).
# The sizes are n, 2n and 4n with a frontier beyond: two rings of 12, 24
# and 48 nodes each, and 64; the ring with chords of 24, 48 and 96 nodes,
# and 128.
#
# READINGS (2026-09-28, CPU 2 under contest_limited.sh, a fresh process per
# reading, the boolean(true) template, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB):
# - weak_head runs around the first cycle and is stopped on every point.
# - tabling(weak_head) completes the cycles: two rings of 12, 24 and 48 in
#   90, 564 and 4909 ms, of 64 stopped at the time limit; the ring with
#   chords of 24, 48 and 96 in 117, 634 and 4773 ms, of 128 stopped at the
#   time limit. The 48- and 96-node points are at the edge: in another run
#   they were stopped near 5 s.
# - records_routed(record_interned(tabling(weak_head)), weak_head) reads
#   within a few per cent of tabling(weak_head) and is stopped on the same
#   two frontier points.
# MEMORY OUTSIDE THE LIMITS. A tabled reading of this family holds far
# more memory than its tables and stacks: at 48 nodes of two rings the
# process's peak resident set was 645 MB while the tables used 17 MB and
# the stacks 40 MB, at 24 nodes 138 MB, and at 96 nodes the 3 GB
# cgroup killed the process (exit 137) before the time limit, so the
# configured limits do not bound it. The cause (measured,
# on the evaluator of that time, deleted in a3b1c52e9): its
# hash_consing store, a trie of the process, keyed a closure by its whole
# code tree, so it grew with the program's size times its lambda nesting
# depth, 489 MB of the 48-node reading. The frontier is set where the time
# limit stops the reading first: 64 and 128 nodes, peak 1.3 GB.
# BEYOND THE FRONTIER (two settings read two rings of 64): one step beyond,
# two rings of 80. At 96, under weak_head the point was killed by the 3 GB
# cgroup in about 2 s (exit 137, 2026-09-28), before the time limit, so
# it would measure the memory outside the limits, not a reading.
# Two rings of 80: weak_head and tabling(weak_head) stopped at the time
# limit, in 6450 and 6579 ms, not killed.
# LIMITS: the family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - breadth-first or depth-first search with a set of visited nodes: J.
#   Hopcroft, R. Tarjan, Algorithm 447: Efficient Algorithms for Graph
#   Manipulation, CACM 1973, doi:10.1145/362248.362272; D. J. King, J.
#   Launchbury, Structuring Depth-First Search Algorithms in Haskell, POPL
#   1995, doi:10.1145/199448.199530.
# - tabled evaluation: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with
#   Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597.

#   THE POINTS.
#
